## Notes for the instruction author

(Delete this section before committing.)

A good iOS instruction file:
- Has an `applyTo:` glob that matches actual file paths in your project (verify with `find . -path ...`)
- Each rule has a concrete "Why" (past production incident or hard invariant) and "How to apply"
- ≤ 150 lines — well inside the documented budget (about two pages / ~1,000 lines per file; the old "code review reads only the first ~4,000 chars" sentence is no longer on the docs, verified 2026-08-22)
- Front-loads the most-violated rules
- Cites at most 3 cross-links

A bad iOS instruction file:
- Aspirational rules ("we should always use SwiftUI") with no enforcement mechanism
- Style preferences (lint config does this)
- Contradicts another instruction file's `applyTo:` glob
- > 150 lines (split into separate files — see the budget note above)
